From 779d4bf0aa5de1d624dafc536e5c33a348a6409f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicholas DeTello <no-reply@detello.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:47:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/11] platform/x86: acer-wmi: Accept gaming WMI results
 larger than 8 bytes

WMI_gaming_execute_u64() accepts result buffers of exactly 4 or 8 bytes,
while WMI_gaming_execute_u32_u64() already accepts buffers of at least
8 bytes. Some firmware declares larger outputs: on the Predator
PT516-52s (BIOS V1.04) the binary MOF describes GetGamingLED as

  void GetGamingLED([in] uint32 gmInput, [out] uint8 gmReturn,
                    [out] uint8 gmOutput[11]);

and the ACPI method returns a 12-byte buffer ({status, state, ...}), so
reading the turbo LED state fails.

Read the first 8 bytes of any buffer that is at least 8 bytes long.

No currently supported model is known to return more than 8 bytes, so
this only matters together with the following patches.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/platform/x86/acer-wmi.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/platform/x86/acer-wmi.c b/drivers/platform/x86/acer-wmi.c
index 40677f82c..c0443b9be 100644
--- a/drivers/platform/x86/acer-wmi.c
+++ b/drivers/platform/x86/acer-wmi.c
@@ -1598,7 +1598,7 @@ WMI_gaming_execute_u64(u32 method_id, u64 in, u64 *out)
 	case ACPI_TYPE_BUFFER:
 		if (obj->buffer.length == sizeof(u32))
 			*out = get_unaligned_le32(obj->buffer.pointer);
-		else if (obj->buffer.length == sizeof(u64))
+		else if (obj->buffer.length >= sizeof(u64))
 			*out = get_unaligned_le64(obj->buffer.pointer);
 		else
 			status = AE_ERROR;
